# Runtime, Tracing, And Replay

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"},
  {:runic_ai, path: "~/wrk/runic_ai"}
])

Code.require_file("support/runic_ai_livebook.exs", __DIR__)
```

## Purpose

RunicAI's runtime and observability pieces exist because agent workflows need to
be operable:

- harness sessions collect inputs, outputs, state, and policy decisions
- runtime sessions give workflows addressable process ownership
- trace events describe what happened semantically
- replay turns a captured trajectory into a regression artifact
- evaluation runs aggregate behavior over cases

This notebook uses the harness and evaluation layer as the easiest way to see
the operational shape.

## Provider Setup

```elixir
setup = RunicAI.Livebook.Setup.inputs()
```

```elixir
model = RunicAI.Livebook.Setup.read_model!(setup)
llm_opts = RunicAI.Livebook.Setup.llm_opts(model, max_output_tokens: RunicAI.Livebook.Setup.read_max_output_tokens(setup))
```

## Build A Turn Processor Session

```elixir
RunicAI.Livebook.Setup.require_openai_key!()

alias RunicAI.Harness.{Policy, TurnProcessor}
alias RunicAI.Recipes.ChatAgentKernel

session =
  TurnProcessor.new_session(ChatAgentKernel,
    recipe_opts: [
      model: model,
      system: "You are concise and observable.",
      llm_opts: llm_opts
    ],
    policy: Policy.new!(max_reactions: 4),
    metadata: %{notebook: "runtime-tracing-replay"}
  )

session =
  session
  |> TurnProcessor.submit(%{user: "Say hello and mention observability."})
  |> TurnProcessor.drain(Policy.new!(max_reactions: 4))

session
```

```elixir
Kino.Layout.tabs(
  Session: Kino.Tree.new(session),
  Outputs: Kino.Tree.new(session.outputs),
  State: Kino.Tree.new(session.state)
)
```

## Evaluation As Release Evidence

Evaluation runs are useful because they produce a compact snapshot that can be
stored with release artifacts.

```elixir
alias RunicAI.Evaluation.Case
alias RunicAI.Recipes.EvaluationHarness

cases = [
  Case.new!(
    id: "observability-mention",
    input: %{user: "Say hello and mention observability."},
    expected: [{:contains_text, "observability"}]
  )
]

run =
  EvaluationHarness.run(cases,
    recipe: ChatAgentKernel,
    recipe_opts: [
      model: model,
      system: "You are concise and include requested terms literally.",
      llm_opts: llm_opts
    ],
    policy: Policy.new!(max_reactions: 4)
  )

Kino.Layout.tabs(
  Run: Kino.Tree.new(run),
  Results: Kino.DataTable.new(Enum.map(run.results, &Map.take(&1, [:case_id, :score, :passed?]))),
  Aggregate: Kino.Tree.new(run.aggregate)
)
```

## Workflow Visualization

```elixir
eval_workflow =
  EvaluationHarness.run_workflow(cases,
    recipe: ChatAgentKernel,
    recipe_opts: [
      model: model,
      system: "You are concise and include requested terms literally.",
      llm_opts: llm_opts
    ],
    policy: Policy.new!(max_reactions: 4)
  )

RunicAI.Livebook.WorkflowViz.tabs(eval_workflow)
```

## Operational Takeaway

The same recipe that runs in a notebook can run through a harness, an integrated
runtime, or a runner-backed session. RunicAI's contracts give you durable
objects to log, replay, score, and inspect without binding your application to
one process topology.
